    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common presenting complaint of an adult patient (or their bed partner) with Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Insomnia", "Loud, disruptive, and often irregular snoring", "Sleepwalking", "Night terrors", "Restless legs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Loud, disruptive snoring, often punctuated by pauses (apneas) and sudden loud gasps or choking sounds, is the classic hallmark symptom reported by the bed partner.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Aside from snoring, what is the most common daytime symptom reported by adult patients with untreated OSA?', '["Hyperactivity", "Excessive Daytime Sleepiness (EDS) and unrefreshing sleep", "Severe anxiety", "Visual hallucinations", "Chronic hiccups"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because their sleep architecture is severely fragmented by hundreds of micro-arousals, the patient never achieves restorative deep sleep, leading to profound daytime fatigue and sleepiness.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient states, "I sleep for 9 hours every night, but I wake up feeling like I was hit by a truck, and I need three cups of coffee just to function." What sleep disorder must be at the top of the differential diagnosis?', '["Narcolepsy", "Obstructive Sleep Apnea", "Delayed Sleep Phase Syndrome", "Insomnia", "Restless Legs Syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Unrefreshing sleep despite adequate sleep duration is a massive red flag for OSA. The brain is not resting; it is fighting for air all night.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does the clinical presentation of severe Obstructive Sleep Apnea typically differ between adults and young children?', '["Children present with EDS; adults present with hyperactivity", "Children often present with hyperactivity, poor school performance, behavioral issues (mimicking ADHD), and secondary nocturnal enuresis (bedwetting); adults present with EDS", "There is no difference in presentation", "Children only present with weight gain", "Adults outgrow their symptoms"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Instead of becoming sleepy, young children with OSA often become paradoxically hyperactive, aggressive, or inattentive. Many are misdiagnosed with ADHD. Bedwetting is also a classic sign in children due to altered sleep architecture and hormonal changes.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old male is being screened for OSA. He complains of waking up multiple times a night to urinate (nocturia). His prostate exam is normal. How does OSA cause nocturia?', '["OSA paralyzes the bladder sphincter", "The negative intrathoracic pressure increases venous return to the heart, tricking the right atrium into thinking the body is fluid-overloaded. This stimulates the release of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP), causing the kidneys to excrete water", "OSA causes diabetes, leading to polyuria", "OSA causes prostate enlargement", "OSA causes vivid dreams about water"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nocturia is a very common, yet often overlooked, symptom of OSA. Fixing the OSA with CPAP typically stops the release of ANP, and the nocturia resolves immediately.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The STOP-BANG questionnaire is a widely used screening tool for OSA. What does the "B" stand for?', '["Blood pressure", "BMI (Body Mass Index) greater than 35 kg/m2", "Breathing rate", "Blood sugar", "Bed partner"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The STOP-BANG criteria are: Snoring, Tired, Observed apneas, Blood Pressure (high), BMI (>35), Age (>50), Neck circumference (>40cm), Gender (Male). A score of 3 or more indicates high risk.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following STOP-BANG scores indicates a high probability of moderate-to-severe Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["0", "1", "2", "5 or greater", "A negative score"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A score of 0-2 is low risk, 3-4 is intermediate risk, and 5-8 indicates a high risk of moderate-to-severe OSA, mandating a sleep study.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'The Epworth Sleepiness Scale (ESS) is a subjective questionnaire used to measure what?', '["The loudness of the patient''s snoring", "The patient''s general level of daytime sleepiness or their propensity to fall asleep in 8 specific sedentary situations", "The number of apneas per night", "The severity of their oxygen desaturation", "The size of their airway"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ESS asks patients to rate their likelihood of dozing off (from 0 to 3) in situations like watching TV, reading, or sitting in traffic. It does NOT diagnose OSA, but quantifies the resulting daytime impairment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What score on the Epworth Sleepiness Scale (ESS) is generally considered abnormal, indicating Excessive Daytime Sleepiness (EDS)?', '["A score greater than 2", "A score greater than 5", "A score greater than 10 (out of 24)", "A score of exactly 24", "A negative score"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A score of 0-10 is considered normal. 11-14 is mild sleepiness, 15-17 is moderate, and 18-24 is severe excessive daytime sleepiness.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following patients should NOT be screened with a Home Sleep Apnea Test (HSAT / Type III monitor), but instead requires an in-lab, attended Polysomnography (Level I)?', '["A healthy 40-year-old male with loud snoring and a high STOP-BANG score", "A 35-year-old female with suspected mild OSA", "A patient with significant comorbid conditions such as severe heart failure, severe COPD, neuromuscular disease, or suspected Central Sleep Apnea", "A patient who lives far from the sleep lab", "A patient who prefers to sleep in their own bed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'HSATs are excellent for diagnosing uncomplicated, moderate-to-severe OSA in healthy individuals. However, they lack the sensors (EEG, advanced respiratory effort belts) needed to accurately diagnose complex sleep breathing disorders or rule out other sleep pathologies in medically complicated patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "gold standard" diagnostic test for Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["A CT scan of the neck", "Drug-Induced Sleep Endoscopy (DISE)", "Level I, in-lab, attended, overnight Polysomnography (PSG)", "An Epworth Sleepiness Scale", "A Home Sleep Apnea Test (HSAT)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An in-lab PSG is the definitive test. It monitors brain waves (EEG) to precisely stage sleep and detect every micro-arousal, providing the most accurate AHI and oxygen desaturation data.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a Polysomnography (PSG), which sensor is primarily used to detect APNEAS (complete cessation of breathing)?', '["The pulse oximeter", "The EEG leads on the scalp", "The oronasal thermal sensor (thermistor)", "The respiratory effort belts on the chest", "The microphone"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A thermistor detects changes in temperature between inhaled (cool) and exhaled (warm) air. A complete flattening of this signal indicates an apnea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a PSG, which sensor is considered the most accurate for detecting HYPOPNEAS (partial airflow reduction)?', '["The oronasal thermal sensor", "The nasal pressure transducer (cannula)", "The microphone", "The EMG leads on the chin", "The EKG leads"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The nasal pressure transducer is much more sensitive to subtle flow limitations and flattening of the inspiratory curve than a thermistor, making it the standard for scoring hypopneas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does a sleep technician distinguish between an Obstructive Apnea and a Central Apnea during a PSG?', '["By looking at the oxygen saturation", "By looking at the EEG", "By looking at the respiratory effort belts (plethysmography) on the chest and abdomen; effort continues during an obstructive event but ceases during a central event", "By listening to the snoring microphone", "By looking at the heart rate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In an obstructive apnea, the patient is trying to breathe, so the chest and abdomen belts show continued (often paradoxical) movement. In a central apnea, the brain signal stops, so the belts are completely flat.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What does the presence of "Paradoxical breathing" on the respiratory effort belts during a PSG signify?', '["The patient is awake", "The patient has central sleep apnea", "Severe upper airway obstruction; as the diaphragm forcefully descends (expanding the abdomen), the massive negative pressure sucks the floppy chest wall inward", "The patient is hyperventilating", "The belts are broken"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Normal breathing expands the chest and abdomen together. Paradoxical breathing (chest goes in while abdomen goes out) is a hallmark of severe inspiratory effort against a closed airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the purpose of the Electroencephalogram (EEG) leads during a Level I Polysomnography?', '["To measure oxygen levels in the brain", "To measure the airflow", "To accurately determine sleep vs. wakefulness, to stage sleep (N1, N2, N3, REM), and to precisely detect cortical arousals caused by respiratory events", "To detect snoring", "To shock the patient if they stop breathing"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Without an EEG (as in most Home Sleep Tests), you cannot know exactly how many minutes the patient actually slept, nor can you score arousals. You can only estimate based on movement and heart rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why does a Home Sleep Apnea Test (HSAT) frequently UNDERESTIMATE the true severity of a patient''s sleep apnea compared to an in-lab PSG?', '["Because the patient sleeps better at home", "Because the HSAT calculates events per hour of RECORDING time, not true SLEEP time (since it lacks an EEG to prove the patient was actually asleep)", "Because HSATs are overly sensitive", "Because HSATs cannot measure oxygen", "Because HSATs only measure snoring"]'::jsonb, 1, 'If an HSAT records for 8 hours, but the patient only slept for 4 hours and had 40 apneas, the true AHI is 10. But the HSAT calculates 40 events / 8 hours = an AHI of 5. This artificially lowers the score.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the diagnostic threshold for Obstructive Sleep Apnea in an adult according to the Apnea-Hypopnea Index (AHI)?', '["An AHI of 1 or greater", "An AHI of 5 or greater (with associated symptoms/comorbidities), or an AHI of 15 or greater regardless of symptoms", "An AHI of 30 or greater", "Any snoring", "An oxygen saturation below 90%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An AHI ≥ 5 with symptoms (EDS, fatigue, witnessed apneas) or comorbidities (hypertension) is diagnostic of OSA. An AHI ≥ 15 is diagnostic even if the patient claims to be completely asymptomatic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Based on the AHI, what constitutes MODERATE Obstructive Sleep Apnea in an adult?', '["AHI 1 to 5", "AHI 5 to 14.9", "AHI 15 to 29.9", "AHI 30 or greater", "AHI over 50"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The standard grading scale is: Mild (5-14.9), Moderate (15-29.9), Severe (≥30).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient returns from their sleep study. Their AHI is 45. What is the severity of their Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Normal", "Mild", "Moderate", "Severe", "Life-threatening"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An AHI of 30 or greater is classified as Severe OSA. This means the patient stops breathing, on average, every 2 minutes or less.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In pediatric sleep medicine, the criteria for diagnosing OSA are much stricter. What AHI threshold is generally considered diagnostic for Obstructive Sleep Apnea in a child (e.g., a 5-year-old)?', '["AHI > 5", "AHI > 15", "AHI > 1 (or > 1.5 depending on the exact guideline), because children have very little respiratory reserve and even mild obstruction is pathologically significant", "AHI > 30", "Children do not get sleep apnea"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Children should not have apneas. An AHI greater than 1 event per hour is considered abnormal in a pediatric sleep study, and an AHI > 5 is considered severe.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is Upper Airway Resistance Syndrome (UARS)?', '["A severe form of Central Sleep Apnea", "A condition where the patient has classic symptoms of OSA (EDS, fatigue), but their sleep study shows a normal AHI and normal oxygen levels. Instead, they have frequent RERAs (arousals driven by increased breathing effort)", "A condition where the airway is completely blocked by a tumor", "A type of asthma", "A condition caused by nasal polyps only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In UARS, the airway doesn''t fully collapse (so no apneas/hypopneas are scored), but it narrows enough that the brain has to constantly wake the patient up to pull air through it. The sleep fragmentation causes severe fatigue despite a "normal" AHI.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How is Upper Airway Resistance Syndrome (UARS) typically treated?', '["With high-dose systemic steroids", "Exactly the same as OSA (e.g., CPAP, oral appliances, or upper airway surgery) because the underlying pathophysiology (airway collapsibility) is the same", "With asthma inhalers", "With sedatives to stop the arousals", "It cannot be treated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'UARS is essentially the "mildest" form of the OSA spectrum structurally, but can cause devastating fatigue. Stenting the airway open with CPAP or surgery cures the problem.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a PSG, a patient is noted to have a cluster of severe obstructive apneas and profound oxygen desaturations specifically occurring every 90-120 minutes throughout the night. What is the most likely explanation?', '["The CPAP machine is malfunctioning on a timer", "The patient is entering REM sleep, where muscle atonia makes the airway maximally collapsible", "The patient is having seizures", "The patient is rolling onto their stomach", "The room temperature is changing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'REM sleep occurs in cycles of approximately 90-120 minutes. Because of REM-associated muscle paralysis, apneas are almost always longest and deepest during these periods.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is meant by the term "Positional OSA"?', '["The patient only has apneas when standing up", "The patient''s AHI is significantly worse (often at least twice as high) when sleeping in the supine (on the back) position compared to the non-supine (lateral or prone) positions", "The patient only has apneas when their head is elevated", "The patient has apneas regardless of position", "The patient stops breathing when they turn their head left"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Positional therapy (using special pillows or wearable devices to force the patient to sleep on their side) can be highly effective or even curative for patients with strictly positional OSA.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A PSG report lists an "Oxygen Desaturation Index (ODI)" of 20. What does this mean?', '["The patient''s oxygen level dropped to 20%", "The patient''s oxygen level dropped below baseline 20 times per hour of sleep", "The patient needs 20 liters of oxygen per minute", "The patient''s oxygen is perfectly normal", "The patient has a 20% risk of a heart attack"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ODI is the number of times per hour the blood oxygen drops by a certain threshold (usually 3% or 4%). It often closely mirrors the AHI but specifically highlights the hypoxic burden.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following describes the "Nadir SpO2" on a sleep study report?', '["The average oxygen saturation for the whole night", "The highest oxygen saturation achieved", "The absolute lowest single blood oxygen saturation level recorded during the entire sleep study", "The time it takes to desaturate", "The level of CO2 in the blood"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The nadir SpO2 highlights the severity of the worst hypoxic event. A nadir of 88% is mild, while a nadir of 65% indicates a severe, life-threatening hypoxic burden on the heart and brain.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient brings in a report from a pulse oximeter they bought at the pharmacy and wore overnight. It shows multiple drops in oxygen. Can this be used to definitively diagnose Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Yes, it is the gold standard", "Yes, if the drops go below 90%", "No, pulse oximetry alone cannot distinguish between OSA, Central Sleep Apnea, hypoventilation, or artifact. It requires correlation with airflow and effort sensors to make a diagnosis", "Yes, but only in children", "No, pulse oximeters don''t work at night"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Overnight oximetry is a screening tool, not a diagnostic one. A drop in oxygen could be a slipped sensor, an asthma attack, heart failure, or an apnea. You must measure airflow to diagnose an apnea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the role of the Electromyogram (EMG) leads placed on the chin (submental area) during a PSG?', '["To measure jaw movement", "To detect teeth grinding (bruxism) and to help identify REM sleep (which is characterized by profound loss of chin muscle tone)", "To detect snoring", "To shock the jaw open if it closes", "To measure the strength of the tongue"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The submental EMG is crucial for sleep staging. The sudden drop to near-zero muscle tone confirms the patient has entered REM sleep. It also spikes during an arousal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with OSA. Their sleep study shows an AHI of 40. During the second half of the study, the technician applies CPAP and increases the pressure. At a pressure of 10 cm H2O, the apneas, hypopneas, and snoring completely stop. What is this process called?', '["A CPAP failure", "A Split-Night PSG with CPAP titration", "A diagnostic-only PSG", "A Multiple Sleep Latency Test (MSLT)", "A Maintenance of Wakefulness Test (MWT)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A "split-night" study is highly efficient. The first half diagnoses the severity of the OSA, and the second half acts as a therapeutic trial to find the exact CPAP pressure required to stent the airway open (the optimal pressure).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a CPAP titration, what is the goal "optimal pressure"?', '["The highest pressure the machine can deliver", "The lowest pressure possible", "The minimum pressure required to eliminate all apneas, hypopneas, snoring, and RERAs in all sleep stages (including REM) and all sleep positions (including supine)", "The pressure that makes the patient stop dreaming", "The pressure that prevents all movement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The optimal pressure provides a perfect pneumatic splint. Since the airway is floppiest during supine REM sleep, the pressure must be titrated to hold the airway open during this most vulnerable state.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with an AHI of 60 complains that they frequently wake up gasping and feel like their heart is racing. What is the physiological cause of the racing heart at the end of an apneic event?', '["The CPAP machine is too loud", "The profound hypoxia and hypercapnia trigger a massive sympathetic nervous system surge (release of epinephrine), causing a sudden tachycardia and spike in blood pressure to wake the brain and open the airway", "The patient is having a heart attack every time", "The parasympathetic system is overactive", "The patient is having a panic attack"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The brain senses it is suffocating and hits the "panic button" (sympathetic surge). This nightly barrage of adrenaline is what ruins the cardiovascular system over time.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which physical exam finding of the oral cavity is most highly correlated with the presence of Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["A missing tooth", "A bifid uvula", "A \"crowded\" or narrow posterior oropharyngeal airway, often assessed using the modified Mallampati or Friedman grading system", "A geographic tongue", "Palatal petechiae"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A high Mallampati score (e.g., Class III or IV, where you can only see the hard and soft palate, but not the uvula or tonsillar pillars) indicates a large tongue relative to the oral cavity, severely crowding the airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "Friedman Palate Position" grading system specifically designed to evaluate in the context of OSA?', '["The risk of vocal cord paralysis", "The size of the tonsils", "The anatomical position of the tongue base relative to the soft palate/uvula, helping to predict the success of palatal surgery (like UPPP)", "The strength of the jaw", "The length of the nose"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dr. Friedman popularized this system. A Friedman Stage I patient (small tonsils, visible uvula) has a high success rate with UPPP, whereas a Stage III/IV patient (hidden uvula, large tongue base) will likely fail UPPP because the obstruction is lower down (retrolingual).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 30-year-old male with a BMI of 24 presents with loud snoring and witnessed apneas. His tonsils are grade 1 (small). When you ask him to bite down, you notice a severe overbite and his chin is visibly recessed. What is the most likely anatomical driver of his OSA?', '["Obesity", "Enlarged adenoids", "Retrognathia/Micrognathia (a retropositioned mandible pushing the tongue base into the airway)", "A deviated septum", "A long uvula"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In non-obese patients, craniofacial skeletal abnormalities (like a small or recessed jaw) are the primary culprits for crowding the airway space.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which cephalometric measurement, obtained from a lateral head X-ray, is classically evaluated to determine the posterior airway space (PAS) behind the base of the tongue?', '["The distance from the tip of the nose to the chin", "The distance from the base of the tongue to the posterior pharyngeal wall", "The angle of the jaw", "The length of the hard palate", "The size of the sinuses"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The PAS is a critical measurement. A PAS less than 11 mm is considered narrow and highly suggestive of retrolingual obstruction.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA notes that his symptoms are significantly worse during allergy season. Why does allergic rhinitis exacerbate OSA?', '["It causes the tonsils to grow", "It causes vocal cord swelling", "Nasal congestion increases upstream airway resistance, forcing higher negative inspiratory pressures which pull the pharyngeal walls inward and cause collapse (Starling resistor effect)", "It causes central sleep apnea", "It causes weight gain"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A blocked nose acts like a pinched straw. The lungs have to suck harder, creating a massive vacuum in the throat that collapses the floppy pharyngeal tissues.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "Berlin Questionnaire"?', '["A test for narcolepsy", "A surgical grading system", "A validated, self-reported screening tool for OSA that focuses on snoring, daytime sleepiness, and the presence of obesity or hypertension", "A psychological test for insomnia", "A machine used to test CPAP pressure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Like the STOP-BANG, the Berlin Questionnaire is a widely used primary care screening tool to stratify a patient''s risk for OSA before ordering a sleep study.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient describes experiencing "sleep paralysis" (waking up unable to move) and "hypnagogic hallucinations" (seeing things while falling asleep), along with severe daytime sleepiness. While they snore mildly, what alternative diagnosis must be considered?', '["Severe OSA", "Narcolepsy", "Schizophrenia", "Bipolar disorder", "Night terrors"]'::jsonb, 1, 'These are classic symptoms of the narcolepsy tetrad (EDS, cataplexy, sleep paralysis, hypnagogic hallucinations). While OSA can coexist, these specific REM-intrusion symptoms suggest a primary central hypersomnia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'If a patient is suspected of having Narcolepsy, what specialized sleep test must be ordered the day AFTER their overnight PSG?', '["A home sleep test", "A Multiple Sleep Latency Test (MSLT) to measure how quickly they fall asleep in a quiet, dark room during the day and if they enter REM sleep abnormally fast", "An EEG of the brain while awake", "A drug-induced sleep endoscopy", "A 24-hour Holter monitor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The MSLT is the gold standard for narcolepsy. Narcoleptics fall asleep very rapidly (mean sleep latency < 8 minutes) and show Sleep-Onset REM Periods (SOREMPs).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA is initiated on CPAP therapy. At their 1-month follow-up, their machine data shows they are using it 7 hours a night, and their AHI is 1.0. However, they complain they are still exhausted during the day. What is the next logical step?', '["Increase the CPAP pressure", "Perform surgery immediately", "Tell them it is in their head", "Ensure they are getting enough total hours of sleep (rule out insufficient sleep syndrome) and consider evaluating for a co-morbid sleep disorder like restless legs or narcolepsy", "Stop the CPAP"]'::jsonb, 3, 'If the CPAP data proves the airway is open (AHI = 1) and they are compliant, but they are still tired, the CPAP is working, but something else is wrong. They might just be sleep-deprived (only sleeping 5 hours) or have another issue.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is NOT a standard parameter recorded during a Home Sleep Apnea Test (Type III monitor)?', '["Nasal airflow via cannula", "Blood oxygen saturation (SpO2)", "Respiratory effort via chest belt", "Electroencephalography (EEG) to stage sleep", "Heart rate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'By definition, a standard Type III home monitor does not use EEG leads. It assumes the patient is asleep based on a lack of movement or respiratory patterns, which is why it can underestimate the AHI.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A pediatric patient with OSA is found to have a highly arched, narrow hard palate and a crossbite. What is the pathophysiologic link between this dental finding and OSA?', '["It is a coincidence", "A narrow palate restricts the size of the nasal floor, causing chronic nasal obstruction, and leaves less room in the oral cavity for the tongue, forcing the tongue backward into the airway", "It causes the tonsils to swell", "It paralyzes the palate", "It causes central apneas"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Maxillary hypoplasia (narrow palate) is a major structural driver of pediatric OSA. Rapid Maxillary Expansion (RME) by an orthodontist can physically widen the nasal floor and oral cavity, often curing the OSA.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a PSG, a patient experiences a 15-second pause in airflow. During this pause, the chest and abdominal belts show no movement at all. At exactly 16 seconds, airflow and chest movement resume simultaneously. How should this event be scored?', '["An obstructive apnea", "A central apnea", "A mixed apnea", "A hypopnea", "A normal breath hold"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The complete absence of respiratory effort during the pause in airflow defines a central apnea. The brain simply didn''t send the signal to the diaphragm.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient experiences a 20-second pause in airflow. For the first 10 seconds, there is no chest movement. For the last 10 seconds, the chest begins heaving violently against a closed airway until the patient wakes up. How should this event be scored?', '["An obstructive apnea", "A central apnea", "A mixed apnea", "A hypopnea", "A RERA"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A mixed apnea begins as a central event (no effort) and ends as an obstructive event (effort against a closed airway). The upper airway collapses during the central pause, so when the brain finally sends the signal to breathe, the airway is glued shut.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "First Night Effect" commonly seen during an in-lab Polysomnography?', '["The patient is cured of their OSA on the first night", "The patient refuses to sleep", "The patient''s sleep architecture is abnormally altered (more wakefulness, less deep sleep, less REM) simply because they are sleeping in a strange, uncomfortable environment covered in wires", "The patient sleepwalks", "The machine breaks"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The strange environment can make it difficult for the patient to achieve REM sleep. Since OSA is often worse in REM, the First Night Effect can sometimes cause the study to underestimate the severity of the OSA.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which metric from a PSG report is most predictive of long-term cardiovascular complications (like stroke or myocardial infarction)?', '["The number of snores", "The duration of Stage N1 sleep", "The severity of the hypoxic burden (e.g., severe Oxygen Desaturation Index, long duration of time spent with SpO2 < 90%)", "The number of leg movements", "The time it took to fall asleep"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The depth and duration of hypoxia (the hypoxic burden) is the primary driver of oxidative stress, endothelial dysfunction, and atherosclerosis in OSA patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old male with a BMI of 40 is diagnosed with severe OSA. He asks if he really needs a CPAP or if he can just "lose some weight." What is the most appropriate medical advice?', '["Weight loss alone is an immediate cure, so CPAP is not needed", "Weight loss is highly recommended and can significantly improve or even cure OSA, but it takes time. CPAP must be initiated immediately to protect his heart and brain while he attempts to lose weight", "Weight loss has no effect on OSA", "Surgery is the only option", "CPAP causes weight gain"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Weight loss is a fantastic long-term goal, but severe OSA is an immediate threat. CPAP provides instant protection. If he loses significant weight (e.g., 50 lbs), another sleep study can be done to see if the CPAP is still needed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is considered a "cardinal symptom" of Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Vivid dreaming", "Witnessed apneas (the bed partner explicitly observing the patient stop breathing and choke/gasp for air)", "Sleep talking", "Teeth grinding", "Restless legs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While snoring is common, a bed partner reporting "he stops breathing and turns blue, then gasps" is highly specific for OSA and is the most reliable historical indicator of the disease.')
